PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Round 0 split (A:10/11/12; B:1/5/7/9/13/14; C:2/3/4/6; D:8) evolved sharply in round 1 to B consensus (1,2,4,5,7,8,9,10,13,14) vs. A (3,6,12) vs. C (11). Core requirements unchanged: sole two directors + 50/50 voting shares for equal control; Tyler needs personal dividend-eligible shares; Alex needs no personal dividend entitlement (salary route only); non-voting investor class must carry usable economic rights (asset participation on dissolution) or it fails requirement 4. 

B now dominant because it alone authorizes non-voting Class C with asset rights (usable for investors) while allowing A/B tailoring for asymmetric compensation; dividend reversal (Tyler on B, Alex on non-div A) viewed as minor/fixable by assignment. A praised for correct dividend holder (Tyler personal Class A) + holding-company route for Alex + clean directors, but fatally weakened by Class C stripped of all economic rights (unusable to investors). C retains correct Alex(A no-div)/Tyler(B div) split + sole directors but adds unnecessary voting Class D (parity risk) and again zero-rights Class C. D lacks non-voting class entirely; E adds extra directors breaking parity. 

Unresolved tension: whether exact named dividend assignment is fatal (A's strength) or secondary to usable investor economics (B's strength) under OBCA. Lean now shifts to B as the minimal structure meeting all four criteria without fatal defects; prior A edge eroded by round-1 emphasis on investor-class usability. Key preserved evidence: OBCA flexibility on class rights, board parity via sole directors, and economic-rights requirement for silent investors.}

